course: dictionary
lesson: 4906
title: Pictures made with words
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: Imagery means pictures made with words. When a writer describes how something looks, sounds, smells, tastes or feels, you can see it in your mind. «Poems paint pictures with words, called imagery.» The poem is the paint, and your mind makes the picture.
explain: Writers often build imagery with comparisons. A simile says one thing is like another, using like or as. A metaphor says one thing is another. «The sun was a golden coin» is a metaphor: the sun is not really a coin, but the picture of a shiny, round, gold coin shows how bright and warm it looked.
explain: When you read imagery, ask two questions: what picture do these words put in my head, and why did the writer choose that picture? Free verse is poetry without regular rhyme, so its imagery and rhythm do much of the work: «poems use line breaks, imagery and rhythm, not only rhyme.»
fact: Imagery means pictures made with words.
fact: Imagery uses the senses: sights, sounds, smells, tastes and feelings.
fact: A simile uses like or as, and a metaphor says one thing is another.
fact: Imagery can also mean real pictures, such as «satellite imagery», which shows the Earth from space.
fact: A song stuck in your head is called an earworm; scientists call it «involuntary musical imagery».
exercise: «Poems paint pictures with words, called imagery.» What is imagery? | *Pictures made with words | Numbers used in maths | Rhyming words at the end of lines | A kind of paint for walls
exercise: What kind of imagery is «The sun was a golden coin»? | Satellite imagery of the sun | A simile, because it uses the word like | A list of sounds | *A metaphor, because it says the sun is a coin
exercise: Free verse has no regular rhyme. Which other features does the lesson say poems can use? | Long lists of numbers | Capital letters only | Dialogue between two people | *Line breaks, imagery and rhythm
test: «Satellite imagery» shows what? | A list of rules for a game | Words that rhyme with each other | *Pictures of the Earth taken from space | Songs stuck in your head
test: Scientists call a song stuck in your head «involuntary musical imagery». What is it often called? | A simile | *An earworm | A satellite picture | Free verse
exam: Which sentence uses imagery to help the reader see something? | Tom went to the shop on Tuesday. | *The moon hung like a silver plate over the silent lake. | I have a pencil and a ruler. | The lesson began at nine o'clock.
